{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "335216656",
    "title": "Proposed: Tag-Based Environment Promotion (GitFlow)",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/335216656",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 18,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "**Draft proposal** — for review by squad leads + Integration lead before this replaces anything in AGENTS.md or the existing Branching Guide. Prepared by Claude Code, 2026-07-16, on `chore/read-me-cicd-updates`.\n\nDecisions made so far\n---------------------\n\n| Decision | Made |\n| --- | --- |\n| Release cadence: cut a new `release/vX.Y.Z` branch every sprint | 2026-07-16 |\n| Pre-launch versioning starts at `v0.1.0` (MAJOR locked at 0); `v1.0.0` is reserved for go-live | 2026-07-16 |\n| `Test` gets its own automated CI gate, modeled on SIT Gate — not manual-only | 2026-07-16 |\n| Tag protection, the version-guard check, and Release-per-final-tag are adopted immediately — not phased in later | 2026-07-16 |\n\nThe problem with today's model\n------------------------------\n\nOnly **Dev** is continuously tied to `develop` (deploys on every merge) — that part is fine, it's meant to be the shared integration environment. But SIT/UAT/Stage/Prod are promoted by pointing a workflow at a **branch or tag ref** (`workflow_dispatch` with a `ref` input). In practice this breaks down two ways:\n\n1. **PR branches don't survive.** Once a story's PR merges to `develop`, its branch is typically deleted. There's nothing left to point a later deploy at — you'd have to keep branches alive indefinitely just to remember what shipped where, which doesn't scale past a couple of people.\n2. **Environments get forced into lockstep.** If Test, SIT, and UAT all deploy the *same* release branch as it exists right now, they can't run independent testing cycles — a fix landed for UAT is *also* now sitting on whatever branch Test/SIT were pointed at, even if those teams haven't finished their own pass yet.\n\nThe fix: promote immutable tags, not floating branches\n------------------------------------------------------\n\nThis is standard GitFlow practice that's currently missing here: **when a batch of work is ready to test, tag it. Every environment deploys a specific tag, never \"whatever's on a branch right now.\"**\n\n* Tags are **immutable and permanent** — `v0.1.0-rc.1` always means exactly one commit, unlike a PR branch that gets deleted on merge.\n* Each environment can sit on a **different tag at the same time** — Test can be validating `v0.2.0-rc.1` while SIT is still working through `v0.1.0-rc.3` and UAT is signing off `v0.1.0-rc.2`. That's what actually decouples the environments' cadences from each other.\n* **No new tooling required.** Every `deploy-*.yml` workflow already takes a `ref` input via `workflow_dispatch`, and `git checkout` doesn't care whether `ref` is a branch name or a tag name. The gap today is process (nobody's cutting tags), not infrastructure.\n\nTag naming convention\n---------------------\n\nMatches this repo's existing `release/v{version}` branch convention (see `.docs/releaseBranchesRules.md`'s own `release/v0.1.0` example) and standard SemVer.\n\n| Tag pattern | Meaning | Example | Points at | Who creates it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `vX.Y.Z-rc.<N>` | Release candidate — a stabilising snapshot, not yet final. Cheap to cut, iterate freely. | `v0.1.0-rc.1`, `v0.1.0-rc.2` | A commit on `release/vX.Y.Z` | Any developer |\n| `vX.Y.Z` | Final release — the exact candidate that passed every gate. This is what actually promotes through UAT → Stage → Prod. | `v0.1.0` | The `release/vX.Y.Z` commit being merged to `main` | Release manager / tier-1-approver only |\n| `vX.Y.Z-hotfix.<N>` | Emergency fix candidate, outside the normal release train | `v0.1.1-hotfix.1` | A commit on `hotfix/vX.Y.Z` | Release manager / tier-1-approver only |\n\n**X.Y.Z meaning:** **X** (major) — breaking change or major milestone (e.g. a new region launch), rare. **Y** (minor) — new stories/features, the normal per-release bump. **Z** (patch) — bug-fix-only releases / hotfixes.\n\n**Pre-launch: MAJOR starts at** `0`**.** SemVer explicitly reserves `0.y.z` for initial development (\"anything MAY change at any time\"), which fits this project's current phase exactly. Cut one release per sprint as a MINOR bump — sprint 1 is `v0.1.0`, sprint 2 is `v0.2.0`, and so on. PATCH within an already-cut sprint version still means bug-fix-only, same as post-launch. `v1.0.0` **is reserved for go-live** — the first time MAJOR moves off `0` — after which MAJOR reverts to its normal rare-breaking-change meaning.\n\n### Anatomy of a tag — `v0.1.0-rc.2` broken down\n\n| Part | Name | Meaning | Increments when... |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `v` | Prefix | Not a version number itself — a literal marker so the tag is unambiguously \"a version\" and `git tag -l 'v*'` filters cleanly. Not part of SemVer itself, but near-universal on git tags. | Never changes |\n| `0` | **MAJOR** | Breaking change or major milestone. Locked at `0` pre-launch (see note above) — `1` at go-live, then rare thereafter. | At go-live (→1), then only for a new region launch, a checkout-flow replatform, or anything severe enough that every in-flight release branch would need to rebase against it |\n| `1` | **MINOR** | A normal release — new stories/features, backward compatible | Every sprint, when a new `release/vX.Y.Z` branch is cut from `develop` |\n| `0` | **PATCH** | Bug-fix-only release against an already-shipped MAJOR.MINOR | A hotfix after `v0.1.0` has already shipped → the next one is `v0.1.1`, not a new minor |\n| `-rc.2` | **Pre-release identifier** | \"Release candidate\" — this specific build is being validated, not yet final. The number is a simple incrementing counter, not an environment reference. | Each time a fix lands and a new candidate build is cut for the same target version |\n\nThe `-rc` number does **not** correspond to an environment — the same `v0.1.0-rc.1` tag can be deployed to Test today and SIT tomorrow. Which environment tested which candidate is tracked via each `deploy-*.yml`'s GitHub Actions run history, not the tag name. The only other pre-release suffix in this scheme is `-hotfix.<N>` (same pattern, cut from `hotfix/vX.Y.Z` instead of `release/vX.Y.Z`). No suffix at all (plain `vX.Y.Z`) means final, shipped.\n\n**Why the dot before the number (**`-rc.2`**, not** `-rc2`**):** SemVer pre-release identifiers compare numerically only when they're separate dot-separated fields. `rc.10` and `rc.2` compare correctly as numbers (10 > 2). `rc10` and `rc2` compare as one string each and sort alphabetically — `rc10` would incorrectly sort *before* `rc2`. This matters directly for the safe-upward-deploy check below.\n\nFlow\n----\n\nwide4000nonedevelop ──● story A merges\n●\n● story B merges ─┐\n● │ sprint ends → cut release/v0.1.0\n●───────────────────release/v0.1.0\n│\n● tag v0.1.0-rc.1 ──▶ deploy to Test (Test gate must pass before promoting further)\n│\n(Test gate passes, or a fix/release-v0.1.0-\\* PR lands a fix)\n│\n● tag v0.1.0-rc.2 (if a fix landed) ──▶ deploy to SIT\n│\n(SIT signs off, on NewStore's own cadence — doesn't block on UAT/Stage)\n│\n● tag v0.1.0 (final candidate) ──▶ UAT\n│\n(Business sign-off)\n│\n● same tag v0.1.0 ──▶ Stage (booked 2-week E2E window)\n│\n(Contractual perf/E2E sign-off)\n│\nmerge release/v0.1.0 → main, tag v0.1.0 on main too,\nback-merge → develop (per existing GitFlow rules)\n│\n▼\nProd (release manager + canary gate)\n\n**Invariant this model depends on:**`main` always reflects exactly what's live in Prod — nothing merges to `main` except a final tag that has actually passed Stage and been deployed. **Multi-region rollout** (AU canary → flag-flip to remaining regions, per `architecture.md`'s Prod row) is controlled by feature flags within a single deployed version, not by separate per-region tags or deploys — this model deploys one version everywhere; regional exposure is a flag concern, not a tagging concern.\n\n> YellowUpdated — review **Correction (review, 2026-07-18):** the SIT step in this diagram assumes `deploy-sit.yml` becomes tag-triggered. That is **not how it works today** — its real trigger is `workflow_run` off `SIT Gate` completing on a `release/**` branch push, with no relationship to tag creation at all. Treat the SIT step above as the proposed target state, not current behaviour, until Open Question 3 below is decided.\n\nThe Test gate\n-------------\n\nDecided: `Test` gets its own automated CI gate, not a manual-only tier. Its trigger shape is different from SIT Gate's, though, so the mechanics need to differ too:\n\n* **SIT Gate (**`digital-sit.yml`**) runs** ***before*** **deploy** — triggered on push/PR to a `release/**` branch, and `deploy-sit.yml` only fires once that gate workflow completes successfully.\n* `Test` **has no such push/PR event to hang a pre-check on** — it's deliberately on-demand (`workflow_dispatch`), including deploying arbitrary feature branches for a quick look before any release branch exists at all.\n\n**Proposed shape:** run the gate *after* the deploy, as a second job in the same `deploy-test.yml` run — deploy first (the on-demand action stays fast and simple), then immediately run a lighter P0-only Playwright suite against what was just deployed. The workflow run's overall pass/fail becomes the promotability signal: a green run means this tag is safe to push on to SIT; a red run means it isn't, without blocking the ability to trigger a look in the first place. This intentionally skips the bundle-size/contract-tests/Lighthouse checks SIT Gate also runs — those matter more once something's headed toward Stage/Prod, not for an early single-story look.\n\nThis is a design proposal, not yet built — flag if you want it modeled differently (e.g. gate-before-deploy to match SIT exactly, at the cost of losing the fast on-demand deploy).\n\nHow to deploy to a specific environment\n---------------------------------------\n\nAll five `deploy-*.yml` workflows (`test`, `sit`, `stg`, `uat`, `prod`) already take a `ref` input via `workflow_dispatch` — this proposal changes what value you put there (a tag, not a branch), not the workflow itself. `Dev` stays fully automatic (every merge to `develop`). Tags are cut with `npm version` (built into npm) rather than raw `git tag` — it bumps `package.json`'s version and creates a correctly-formatted **annotated** tag in one step, so nobody has to remember the naming rules or the `-a -m` flags by hand. See Tagging management strategy below for why annotated tags matter.\n\n### 1. Cut a release branch at the end of each sprint\n\nwide4000bash\ngit checkout develop && git pull\ngit checkout -b release/v0.1.0\ngit push -u origin release/v0.1.0\n\n### 2. Cut the first release candidate and deploy it to Test (or SIT)\n\nwide4000bash\ngit checkout release/v0.1.0\n# First rc of a pre-launch sprint release -> preminor (MAJOR stays locked at 0).\nnpm version preminor --preid=rc -m \"Release candidate %s\"\n# package.json is now 0.1.0-rc.0, an annotated tag v0.1.0-rc.0 was created, and a\n# \"Release candidate v0.1.0-rc.0\" commit was added on this branch.\ngit push origin release/v0.1.0 --follow-tags\ngh workflow run deploy-test.yml -f ref=v0.1.0-rc.0\n# once the Test gate passes, same tag (or the next rc) goes to SIT\ngh workflow run deploy-sit.yml -f ref=v0.1.0-rc.0\n\n### 3. A problem is found — fix, re-tag, re-deploy just the environment that needs it\n\nwide4000bash\ngit checkout release/v0.1.0\ngit checkout -b fix/release-v0.1.0-checkout-bug\n# ...make the fix, open a PR targeting release/v0.1.0, get it merged...\ngit checkout release/v0.1.0 && git pull\nnpm version prerelease --preid=rc -m \"Release candidate %s\" # 0.1.0-rc.0 -> 0.1.0-rc.1\ngit push origin release/v0.1.0 --follow-tags\ngh workflow run deploy-test.yml -f ref=v0.1.0-rc.1 # re-deploy wherever needed — SIT is untouched if it's still on rc.0\n\n### 4. Every rc has signed off — cut the final tag and promote it through UAT → Stage → Prod\n\nwide4000bash\ngit checkout release/v0.1.0\nnpm version 0.1.0 -m \"Release %s\" # strips the -rc.N suffix explicitly — npm has no automatic \"graduate\" verb\ngit push origin release/v0.1.0 --follow-tags\ngh workflow run deploy-uat.yml -f ref=v0.1.0\n# after Business sign-off:\ngh workflow run deploy-stg.yml -f ref=v0.1.0\n# after the Stage E2E window signs off, merge to main:\ngit checkout main && git merge --no-ff release/v0.1.0\ngit push origin main\ngh workflow run deploy-prod.yml -f ref=v0.1.0\n# back-merge per existing GitFlow rules (.docs/releaseBranchesRules.md)\ngit checkout develop && git merge --no-ff release/v0.1.0\ngit push origin develop\n\n### 5. Rollback — not a special case\n\nIf a tag deployed to Prod turns out to be bad, there's no separate rollback mechanism to build — just redeploy the previous known-good tag:\n\nwide4000bashgh workflow run deploy-prod.yml -f ref=v0.0.1\n\nTracking deployments & safe upward promotion\n--------------------------------------------\n\nHow do we know what's currently live in each environment, and how do we stop someone accidentally deploying an older tag over a newer one?\n\n| Concern | Recommendation |\n| --- | --- |\n| **What's deployed where, right now** | Already tracked for free. Every `deploy-*.yml` job is bound to a real GitHub `environment:`, so GitHub automatically records a Deployment each run — visible at Settings → Environments → `<tier>` → Deployments, or `gh api repos/ZimmermannAU/zimmermann-pwa/deployments?environment=sit`. This exists today; it's just not surfaced anywhere visible to the team yet. |\n| **Preventing an accidental downgrade** | Add immediately (see Decisions above): a version-guard step to each `deploy-*.yml`, before the push step — fetch the environment's current deployed tag via the Deployments API above, semver-compare it against the tag being deployed, and fail on a downgrade. Use the `semver` npm package for the comparison rather than hand-rolled bash string comparison — see the `-rc.2` vs `-rc2` note above for exactly the class of bug that produces. **Use the actual GitHub Environment name, not the workflow-file suffix** — they diverge for `stg` (`deploy-stg.yml` targets the `staging` Environment, not `stg`; this exact mismatch already bit this repo once, see `deploy-stg.yml`'s fix history). Map each tier to its real Environment name explicitly, and fail closed on an unresolvable environment/API error — as opposed to a genuine first-ever deployment with no prior Deployment record at all, which should be allowed to proceed. |\n\nTagging management strategy\n---------------------------\n\n| Concern | Recommendation |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Who can create a tag?** | `-rc` tags: any developer — they're cheap, iterative, and low-risk. Final `vX.Y.Z` and `-hotfix` tags: release manager / tier-1-approver only — mirrors the existing tier-1-approver gate pattern already used for `npm run env -- push` and Contentful config writes. |\n| **Ensuring consistency** | Use `npm version` (see \"How to deploy\" above) instead of hand-typing `git tag` — it's a standard, built-in npm command that enforces correct SemVer formatting and bumps `package.json` in the same step, so the two never drift. Primary enforcement is the GitHub Rulesets described in the \"Tag protection\" row below, which genuinely hard-block a non-matching tag at push time on GitHub.com. A `push: tags: ['*']` validation workflow is worth keeping too, as defense-in-depth and for a clearer failure message than the raw ruleset rejection gives. |\n| **Tracking who created a tag** | `npm version` creates **annotated** tags by default (never lightweight ones) — the tagger's identity, timestamp, and message are embedded directly in the tag object (`git show v0.1.0-rc.2` reveals it). This is why the commands above always pass `-m`. |\n| **Avoiding tag name conflicts** | Mostly already solved by git itself: `git push origin <tag>` fails outright if that tag already exists on the remote — there's no silent overwrite without an explicit `--force`, which the tag-protection rules below block for anyone but repo admins / tier-1-approvers. `npm version prerelease` also always reads the *current* `package.json` version before incrementing, so it can't produce a number that's already been used on that branch. |\n| **Interaction with existing tagging tools** GreenNew — review | Two existing tagging mechanisms predate this proposal and need an explicit resolution, not a silent replacement. `scripts/tagRelease.sh` — the tool the current Branching Guide already prescribes — creates a different tag pair (`v{version}` + `release/digital/v{version}`) via raw `git tag`, run from `main` after a release PR merges; it has no `-rc` concept and conflicts directly with the `npm version`-based flow above. **Recommendation: retire** `tagRelease.sh` **once this proposal is approved** — through the normal script-deprecation process, not a silent deletion. Separately, `deploy-dev.yml`'s `tag-sprint` job pushes `sprint/{sprint}/{date}` tags on every `develop` merge that touches `scripts/sprint.txt` — a distinct namespace (not `v*`) tracking sprint rollover, not release promotion. **No conflict — this coexists unchanged**, since the tag-naming Rulesets below only target `v*`. |\n| **Tag protection & naming enforcement** YellowUpdated — review | Adopted immediately (see Decisions above), but as **two separate GitHub Rulesets**, not one — confirmed against GitHub's own Ruleset docs that a single ruleset has only one bypass list for its whole target pattern, so it cannot grant open `-rc` creation to any developer while simultaneously restricting final/hotfix creation to tier-1-approvers. **Ruleset A** targets `v*-rc.*` (tag name pattern `^v\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+-rc\\.\\d+$`) with \"restrict updates\" and \"restrict deletion\" enabled for immutability, and creation left open to any developer with write access — no bypass list needed. **Ruleset B** targets `v*` excluding `-rc.*` (via the ruleset's include/exclude-by-pattern conditions; tag name pattern `^v\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+(-hotfix\\.\\d+)?$`) with \"restrict creation,\" \"restrict updates,\" and \"restrict deletion\" all enabled, bypass list scoped to `@ZimmermannAU/tier-1-approvers`. Together the two rulesets give the same \"newer and more capable than the old tag-protection-rules UI, works on GitHub.com\" guarantee as the original single-ruleset design — just correctly split the way GitHub actually requires. |\n| **When to tag** | Only at a real promotion gate (about to deploy somewhere), never automatically per-commit — otherwise the tag list becomes as noisy as the commit log and loses its value as a promotion record. |\n| **Retention — do we ever delete old tags?** | Keep everything, indefinitely. Tags cost nothing to retain and deleting them destroys the audit trail this whole model is built on (\"what was actually deployed to SIT on this date\"). If the tag list ever becomes genuinely unwieldy, revisit — don't pre-optimise for it now. |\n| `package.json` **version field** | Currently static at `0.0.1`. Under this proposal it tracks the current sprint's release automatically, since `npm version` writes it as part of cutting every tag — no separate bookkeeping needed. |\n\nRules & automation for AI-assisted development\n----------------------------------------------\n\nThis project is heavily Claude-Code-assisted, so the convention needs to hold up even when an AI agent (not just a human) is the one cutting a tag. Two layers, not interchangeable:\n\n| Layer | What it does | How reliable |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Guidance** — a new `.claude/rules/tagging.md` (or folded into `.docs/releaseBranchesRules.md`, already auto-loaded on `release/*`/`hotfix/*` branches per AGENTS.md) | Documents the convention and tells Claude explicitly to use `npm version`, never construct a `git tag` string by hand | Best-effort — shapes behaviour, but depends on the rule having actually been loaded and followed |\n| **Enforcement** — the tag-naming GitHub Ruleset, the `push: tags: ['*']` validation workflow, and the Test-gate job (see above) | Runs server-side regardless of who or what pushed the tag / triggered the deploy | A real guarantee — catches a mistake even if the guidance was never read |\n\nThe final/hotfix tag gate should reuse the exact `isTier1Approver()` pattern already implemented in `scripts/switchEnv.mjs` — the same trust boundary already used elsewhere in this repo, and one Claude sessions here already know how to check (`gh api orgs/ZimmermannAU/teams/tier-1-approvers/members`).\n\nPer-environment summary\n-----------------------\n\n| Environment | What it deploys today | What it would deploy under this proposal | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Local** | `npm start` against your working tree | *(unchanged)* | — |\n| **Dev** | Every merge to `develop` | *(unchanged — this is correct as-is, it's meant to be the noisy shared integration tier)* | Auto-deploys on every `develop` merge |\n| **Test** *(new —* `deploy-test.yml`*)* | N/A — didn't exist | A feature/fix branch, or an early `-rc` tag, deployed on demand — gated by its own P0-only CI check (see \"The Test gate\" above) before promoting further | `ref: v0.1.0-rc.1`, or even a raw feature branch for a quick look before a release is cut at all |\n| **SIT** YellowUpdated — review | Whatever's on the `release/**` branch when the SIT Gate workflow completes (`deploy-sit.yml` is `workflow_run`-triggered off SIT Gate, not tag-triggered) | A specific `-rc` tag, re-deployed only when a new `-rc` is cut — ***not yet true**, requires a* `deploy-sit.yml` *trigger change from* `workflow_run` *to a tag push; see Open Question 3* | `ref: v0.1.0-rc.2` *(target state, pending decision)* |\n| **UAT** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | The release's final candidate tag | `ref: v0.1.0` |\n| **Stage** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | Same tag UAT is validating, once UAT signs off | `ref: v0.1.0` |\n| **Prod** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | The tag that passed Stage, pushed to `main` | `ref: v0.1.0` |\n\nChangelogs and GitHub Pages\n---------------------------\n\nGitHub Pages is enabled on this repo but has never successfully deployed (confirmed via the API — `status: null`). The only thing currently wired to publish there is `digital-sit.yml`'s Playwright-report job. That matters here because `actions/deploy-pages` is a **single-site-per-repo** model — each deployment fully replaces the previous one. A changelog page and the Playwright report can't both live on Pages independently; they'd need to be combined into one build with internal navigation, which is a real chunk of work on its own.\n\nGiven that, changelogs should go through **GitHub Releases** instead — no new infrastructure needed, and it's already half-built: `.claude/skills/generate-changelog` already ends by offering to run `gh release create [VERSION] --draft --notes '[SUMMARY]'`, and its own base-ref detection already defaults to `git describe --tags --abbrev=0` — the skill was written assuming tags would exist. Adopting this proposal completes something already designed, rather than adding something new. Run `/generate-changelog` at step 4 above (cutting the final tag) to produce the Release. A Pages-hosted changelog UI remains a legitimate future nice-to-have, just not the pragmatic starting point.\n\nWhat changes vs. today\n----------------------\n\n|  | Today | Proposed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What gets deployed | A branch name, or an ad-hoc ref someone remembers | A named, immutable tag |\n| Tracking \"what's where\" | Tribal knowledge / Slack history | GitHub Environments' Deployment history / `git tag -l` / GitHub Releases page |\n| Can Test/SIT/UAT run independent cycles? | No — pointed at the same moving branch | Yes — each on its own tag |\n| New infrastructure needed | — | A Test-tier P0 gate job (new); everything else — `deploy-test.yml`, tag protection, the version-guard, `npm version`, `/generate-changelog` — already exists or is a config change, not new code |\n\nOpen questions for the team\n---------------------------\n\n1. **Test gate design** — is deploy-then-gate (proposed above) acceptable, or should it match SIT's gate-then-deploy pattern exactly, at the cost of the fast on-demand deploy?\n2. **Test gate scope** — P0 Playwright suite only, or should any of bundle-size/contract-tests/Lighthouse also apply at this tier?\n3. **SIT tag-triggering** GreenNew — review *(raised in review)* — this proposal's Flow diagram and per-environment table assume SIT redeploys when a new `-rc` tag is cut, but `deploy-sit.yml`'s real trigger today is `workflow_run` off `SIT Gate` completing on a `release/**` push, with no relationship to tag creation at all. Two options: (a) change `deploy-sit.yml` to trigger on a tag push instead, matching the Test/UAT/Stage/Prod model this proposal builds — a real workflow change, not just a docs update; or (b) keep SIT branch-triggered as-is and stop describing it as tag-scoped in this proposal, treating SIT as an intentional exception to the \"every environment sits on a named tag\" rule. Needs a decision before this proposal is considered final.",
